1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:19,231
By six o'clock, our hotel had become the most expensive bad decision
in the Last City. There were race cars in the courtyard, guns on the
billiard tables, and three women arguing with a florist over whether
white roses were romantic or evidence that a nigga had given up.

2
00:00:19,481 --> 00:00:43,830
Rosa stood on the piano in the ballroom wearing a green dress and
directing furniture like she had conquered the building herself. Tank
held her shoes. The pianist, a grown man with gray hair, kept looking
at her feet as if God had finally answered a very specific prayer.
"Nobody sits with their back to that window," Rosa announced. "Why?"
the owner asked.

3
00:00:44,080 --> 00:01:07,069
"Because I just paid to have my hair done and we in a city full of
shooters." He moved the chairs personally. I came downstairs in black
trousers, a white shirt, my shoulder holster, and the captain's badge
hanging against my chest. The blood money years had taught me how to
dress for dinner. Police work had taught me that the gun should match.

4
00:01:07,319 --> 00:01:27,332
Shawna came down behind me wearing blue. Nigga, some colors belong to
everybody until the right woman walks into them. Then you realize the
rest of the world had only been borrowing. Her dress moved like water
around her legs. Dante's watch sat on her wrist. My ring shone on her
hand.

5
00:01:27,582 --> 00:01:46,504
She looked at the ballroom, then at the crowd looking at her, and
smiled without asking any of them for permission. I forgot the thing I
had been saying. "Captain?" the hotel owner prompted. "Give me a
minute." "Of course." "Maybe two." He understood. Every nigga in the
room understood.

6
00:01:46,754 --> 00:02:11,183
Vesper stood by the doors in burgundy silk, speaking to a silver-
haired woman whose black gown looked capable of inheriting property.
The older woman watched us approach with a small smile. She carried a
cane topped by a pearl. I had seen enough royal women to know the cane
was either a weapon or an excuse to hit somebody with a weapon.
"Aiden," Vesper said.

7
00:02:11,433 --> 00:02:35,573
"Magnolia Saint-Clair. My old tutor." "Not that old," Magnolia said.
"Sixty-four is an excellent age for disapproving of emperors." She
offered me her hand. I kissed it because my mother had raised me
intermittently but effectively. "A captain, a king, and he remembers
manners," Magnolia said. "Vesper, you should have brought him years
ago." "I was busy," I said.

8
00:02:35,823 --> 00:02:59,199
"Doing what?" "Becoming all that shit." She laughed. A clean, wicked
laugh. I liked her immediately. Shawna took her other hand. "I'm his
wife." "Yes, dear. He looked at you as if somebody had opened the sun.
I worked it out." Magnolia led us toward a table under a chandelier
shaped like falling rain. Vesper sat beside her. Shawna sat beside me.

9
00:02:59,449 --> 00:03:24,202
Two queens and a woman who had probably slapped a king with a
dictionary. I had entered the room thinking I was in charge of dinner.
Then Mercedes arrived with champagne in both hands and Porsche behind
her, admitting guests like she had bought the doorway. "The women
downstairs want to know if this is private," Mercedes said. "What
women?" Shawna asked. "Zero's welcome party.

10
00:03:24,452 --> 00:03:51,633
Their bus driver saw Aiden's cars and developed better judgment." A
line of grown women in glittering dresses waited behind Porsche.
Dancers, escorts, a violinist, and one woman carrying a diamond-
wearing dog that had never looked at a price in its fucking life.
"We're happy to be confused," the violinist said. "Nigga, welcome.
Confusion gets a glass. Certainty gets you work." They came in
cheering.

11
00:03:51,883 --> 00:04:15,890
The pianist caught Rosa's eye and found a rhythm. Tank put her shoes
down in front of her with both hands, like the nigga was surrendering
two very beautiful hostages. She took the microphone. "My man says he
can't dance." "Nigga, don't help her," Tank told me. "I'm the police.
The woman made an allegation. We need a demonstration." Rosa held out
her hand.

12
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:41,490
Tank looked at the room. Every nigga suddenly became a witness.
"Nobody film my feet." "Nobody wants to miss them," Porsche said.
"They going in different directions." He went to Rosa. One hand too
high, one foot early, shoulders trying to negotiate a separate song.
Nigga danced like a refrigerator being delivered through a doorway an
inch too narrow. Rosa whispered in his ear.

13
00:04:41,740 --> 00:05:04,487
He laughed. His shoulders dropped. She moved; he found her. No
miracle, just two people choosing the same next step. Shawna leaned
against me. "He's practicing." "For the wedding?" "For being happy
without looking over his shoulder." I looked over mine. She pinched my
leg. "Nigga, you saw him learning and immediately failed the class." I
kissed her hair.

14
00:05:04,737 --> 00:05:32,448
"Somebody gotta watch the door." Magnolia leaned closer when Rosa
began to sing. "Zero wants every hotel to report your visitors."
"Nigga needs a larger notebook." "He recalled the airport landing
permissions too." My phone held eleven messages from Cassius Chrome.
Landed. Cars detained. Photograph of the nigga standing on a crate,
arms folded. Then: BROTHER I HAVE BEEN PATIENT FOR ALMOST NINE
MINUTES.

15
00:05:32,698 --> 00:05:58,755
I called. "Where you at?" "Warehouse beside the airport. They say my
fleet is imperial property." "You show the niggas your paperwork?" "I
showed them my face." "Cassius." "They remained confused. Inez is
clarifying." Somebody yelled behind him. Metal clattered. "Nigga, what
she using?" "Their desk." "Tell her leave me something. I got dressed
for this." I stood up. Shawna stood with me.

16
00:05:59,005 --> 00:06:22,530
Magnolia smiled into her glass. "Dinner before the battle would have
been conventional." "Save our plates," Shawna told her. Vesper rose.
"I know the airport director." "That good?" I asked. "For us." Tank
was still dancing when I reached him. He saw my face, kissed Rosa, and
stepped back into his shoes like somebody had rung the bell on a
champion.

17
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:42,101
"Who we getting?" "Cassius and Inez." "What they do?" "Arrived too
rich." "Nigga, that's discrimination." June came out of the courtyard
with Adrian, her partner. He was thirty-five, lean, with a shaved head
and a little beard that made him look like he had strong opinions
about engines.

18
00:06:42,351 --> 00:07:07,348
Our men had brought him safely through the old service route, along
with three tool chests and, somehow, half a motorcycle. "You going to
the airport?" he asked. "Apparently." "Take the eastern road. Main
gate has an armored checkpoint." June shook her head. "Eastern road
has two." I looked between them. "Which one got better scenery?" They
stared. Shawna picked up my jacket. "Main gate.

19
00:07:07,598 --> 00:07:29,247
He likes an entrance." We took four cars. Me and Shawna in the
Lamborghini. Tank and Rosa in the Mercedes because she had refused to
stay behind while her friends were detained. Vesper rode with Mercedes
and Porsche. June and Adrian brought a vicious little silver coupe
that looked like it had been built during an argument.

20
00:07:29,497 --> 00:07:53,152
At the airport gate, a man in a black uniform held up his hand. I
stopped close enough that he could admire the badge on my hood. "The
airport is closed." "Then open it." "To unauthorized arrivals." "I'm a
departure from your usual bullshit. Move." He looked at Shawna, then
at the cars behind us. A second guard approached holding a tablet.

21
00:07:53,402 --> 00:08:18,349
My photograph was on the screen with a red border around it. "You are
expected," he said. "See? Nigga got the invitation." The barrier rose.
Shawna watched the first guard step aside. "Too easy." "Maybe my smile
working." "It works on me. I still know when you're lying." Inside the
cargo yard, a dozen supercars sat under floodlights. Red, silver,
black, blue.

22
00:08:18,599 --> 00:08:42,480
Cassius stood on the hood of a white truck in a purple racing jacket,
applauding slowly as I drove in. Inez leaned against a yellow car with
her arms folded. Three guards lay on the ground near her, awake and
reconsidering their employment. "My brother!" Cassius called. "You
brought the police!" I got out. "Nigga, I am the police." "Exactly
what I told them.

23
00:08:42,730 --> 00:09:04,491
They asked for a local authority." Vesper stepped out of her car. The
airport director, a round man with a red face, stopped speaking before
he finished turning toward her. "Your Majesty." "Director Pell." "I
had no idea these were your guests." "You had several ideas. Most of
them were poor." She held out her hand for the keys.

24
00:09:04,741 --> 00:09:27,333
Before he could move, the warehouse doors behind him slammed shut.
Floodlights snapped on across the roof. A voice rolled over the yard.
"Captain. I hoped you would bring the whole family." Roland Ruin stood
on a balcony above the loading bays. Of course that nigga had found a
balcony. Men like him believed height was a personality.

25
00:09:27,583 --> 00:09:50,559
He wore his white driving suit again, now open at the throat. A line
of Black Mantle guards appeared beside him. Their rifles pointed down
at us. Cassius looked up, then at me. "I dislike this airport." "One
star," I said. Roland smiled. "You may leave. Your vehicles remain."
"You can keep that ugly truck," I told him.

26
00:09:50,809 --> 00:10:13,190
Cassius looked down at the hood beneath his feet. "It is a rental."
"Even better." A shot struck the pavement by my shoe. The whole yard
moved. Shawna pulled Vesper behind the Lamborghini. Tank shoved Rosa
toward a concrete pillar. I drew and fired at the balcony as Cassius
jumped off the truck. The guards ducked.

27
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:34,154
Inez was already moving between the cars, low and fast, keys flying
from her hand to mine. "Silver one!" she shouted. I caught them
without looking. June's little coupe roared across the yard and struck
a stack of empty pallets. Wood scattered. Adrian leaned out and fired
toward the warehouse windows.

28
00:10:34,404 --> 00:10:57,558
The distraction gave Cassius enough time to pull a door open on the
nearest race car. "Everybody drives!" I yelled. "Aiden!" Shawna
called. I turned. She had taken the Lamborghini's wheel. My wife
looked at the silver car, then at me. "Keep up." Nigga, she drove away
before I could fall more in love. I jumped into the silver car.

29
00:10:57,808 --> 00:11:20,552
It smelled of new leather and expensive gasoline. The engine woke like
a predator hearing its own name. Across the dashboard, a small plaque
read CHROME SPECIAL NUMBER ONE. "Cassius!" I shouted into the radio.
"This yours?" "Was." "What?" "I brought you a gift. Try not to put
bullets in the seats." I laughed and hit the gas.

30
00:11:20,802 --> 00:11:44,183
The car shot forward so hard I felt my stomach stay in customs.
Bullets cracked off the yard around me. I swerved behind a cargo
loader, came out beside the white rental truck, and saw Roland
descending a staircase at the far end of the warehouse. He had a black
racing car waiting below. That nigga thought he was leaving his own
party early.

31
00:11:44,433 --> 00:12:05,218
I swung the silver car around the truck, followed Shawna beneath the
balcony, and cut toward the staircase. Roland fired through his open
door. A bullet took my mirror. I returned two shots through the empty
stretch ahead of him, enough to make him drop into his seat and pull
away before his guard could join him.

32
00:12:05,468 --> 00:12:28,952
Tank rammed the front barrier with a cargo tractor. I did not know
where he had found a cargo tractor. I had stopped asking where Tank
found heavy things. Some men had guardian angels. Mine had industrial
equipment. The barrier folded. Rosa stood beside him in the little
cab, still holding the microphone she had carried out of the ballroom
by mistake.

33
00:12:29,202 --> 00:12:54,591
"Ladies and gentlemen," she shouted into it, "we will be leaving this
raggedy-ass airport!" We left in a river of stolen-back luxury. Roland
took the outer service road. I followed. Shawna came up on my left,
blue police lights spinning over the Lamborghini. Behind her, Cassius
and Inez moved together, two bright blades in the night. A plane
rolled across the taxiway ahead of us.

34
00:12:54,841 --> 00:13:16,058
Roland accelerated toward it. "He going under the wing," Cassius said.
"Then so am I." "Your roof is higher." "Nigga, don't make me nervous
after giving me the car." I lowered my head anyway. The black car
slipped beneath the plane's wing. My silver roof cleared it by so
little I could hear the air change.

35
00:13:16,308 --> 00:13:39,496
On the other side, Roland swung sharply onto a narrow ramp. I
followed, tires singing. Shawna took the wider route around the plane
and came out ahead of us. She blocked the exit. Roland braked. I
closed behind him. For one second we had the nigga between husband and
wife, which is where all his decisions had been trying to put him
since morning.

36
00:13:39,746 --> 00:14:00,035
He looked from her car to mine. Then he drove through a glass terminal
wall. The crash flashed white under the lights. Glass burst into the
empty lounge. His car tore between rows of seats, climbed a shallow
staircase, and vanished through another window into the passenger
pickup lane. I could have followed.

37
00:14:00,285 --> 00:14:22,526
Then I saw the cleaner standing inside the terminal, frozen beside a
cart, with a falling metal sign swinging above him. I stopped. Shawna
stopped too. I ran through the broken wall, grabbed the man by the
shoulder, and pulled him clear as the sign came down hard enough to
fold his cart in half. He clung to my shirt, shaking.

38
00:14:22,776 --> 00:14:44,460
A grown man, fifty maybe, with tired eyes and rubber gloves. "You all
right?" He looked at the wreckage. Then at me. "My cart." "We'll get
you a new one." "It was a good cart." "Nigga, I'm buying you an
excellent cart." He laughed once, shocked to hear himself do it.
Cassius came through the doorway and surveyed the destruction.

39
00:14:44,710 --> 00:15:03,328
"Roland escaped?" "Yeah." He looked at the man in my arms, then
nodded. "Good brakes on that car." "Best part so far." We took the
cleaner with us. His name was Omar. He had a wife at home who,
according to him, would never believe any of this unless she saw the
cars.

40
00:15:03,578 --> 00:15:26,359
So we drove him to the hotel, gave him dinner, and put him in a video
call with his wife while Rosa sang behind him and Tank held the new
car keys against his chest like a jeweler presenting diamonds. His
wife believed him immediately. "Omar," she said, "don't let that
handsome captain leave before I get there." Shawna raised her glass at
the phone.

41
00:15:26,609 --> 00:15:44,156
"You can look." "I have been married thirty years. Looking is my
vacation." The ballroom roared. Omar's wife arrived twenty minutes
later with a handbag big enough to discipline a whole household. She
saw him beside Tank and stopped with both hands on her hips.

42
00:15:44,406 --> 00:16:04,150
"Nigga, you went to clean an airport." "I did clean some of it." "Now
you eating with the nigga on television." "He came through the
window." "Aiden came through your window?" "A car came through first.
Then that nigga came through." Tank nodded. "That's usually how you
know he's close." She looked at me.

43
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:25,203
"Captain, this nigga has been telling me for thirty years that nothing
interesting happens at work." "Ma'am, he was saving it." Omar stood to
embrace her. She hit his arm once, then held him for so long the whole
table learned why the nigga had wanted to get home. When she let go,
he pointed toward the courtyard.

44
00:16:25,453 --> 00:16:50,431
"They got me a new cart." "Nigga, forget the cart. You alive." "It's
got a little motor." She paused. "A motor?" "June fixed it." His wife
looked at June, who lifted her glass without apology. "This nigga
gonna drive it around our kitchen," the woman said. "Only on
weekends," Omar promised. "Nigga, I know you." I leaned toward Shawna.
"That's marriage." She kissed my cheek.

45
00:16:50,681 --> 00:17:13,881
"That is exactly marriage." I sat beside my wife with dust in my hair
and a silver car outside that had been a gift for less than twenty
minutes before surviving an airfield gunfight. Inez stole a piece of
bread off Cassius's plate. He pretended to object, then moved the
whole basket toward her. Magnolia studied them over her wine.

46
00:17:14,131 --> 00:17:37,593
"Another wedding?" "Drive slowly," Inez told her. Cassius smiled into
his glass. The violinist approached our table with her instrument
under one arm and a plate of food in her hand. Up close she had a
little silver scar through one eyebrow and a look that said she had
heard men promise things in several languages. "Sienna," she said.
"Thirty-one.

47
00:17:37,843 --> 00:17:59,882
I play violin, drive boats, and occasionally make rich men believe
they're interesting." "Busy woman," Shawna said. "Depends how rich."
She sat when Shawna moved a chair for her. "Those other women are my
friends. We worked the emperor's private parties. The motor champions
paid, but the palace decided when we could leave.

48
00:18:00,132 --> 00:18:24,834
Tonight I told the driver the wrong address." Mercedes appeared beside
her with fresh glasses. "I knew that nigga hadn't got lost. He took
every turn like he'd rehearsed an escape." "He had," Sienna said. "For
two weeks. Our brother doesn't like leaving either of us behind. My
sister plays cello. She's upstairs changing." I looked toward the door
where the women had first gathered.

49
00:18:25,084 --> 00:18:47,587
The little dog slept on a silk cushion, wearing enough jewelry to
retire a bank manager. One of the dancers was teaching Tank a turn
while Rosa laughed at both of them. Everybody had arrived carrying a
different reason for wanting to stay. "Palace coming for you?" I
asked. "Probably." "Then they know where to find me." Sienna studied
me.

50
00:18:47,837 --> 00:19:09,423
"You always say shit like that?" "Only when I'm awake." She smiled,
but her fingers tightened around the violin's neck. Shawna noticed.
She set her glass down and put a hand over Sienna's. "There's enough
room upstairs," my wife said. "You and your friends decide what you
want tomorrow. Tonight you eat." Sienna looked at her for a moment.

51
00:19:09,673 --> 00:19:34,522
Then she began eating, fast at first, until she remembered nobody was
taking the plate. The pianist asked whether she would play with Rosa.
She swallowed, wiped her mouth, and stood. "One song. Then I want
cake." Nigga, that woman put the whole desert inside four strings. The
ballroom quieted. Her sister came down with the cello and joined
before anyone could ask.

52
00:19:34,772 --> 00:19:55,734
Rosa listened for a verse, then found a melody above them, her voice
low and rough and full of everything she did not usually explain. Tank
stopped moving. I watched him watch her. His big hands rested on the
back of a chair. He looked almost frightened by how much he loved the
woman singing under that chandelier.

53
00:19:55,984 --> 00:20:18,260
"You good?" I asked when he came beside me. "Yeah." "Your face look
otherwise." He rubbed his jaw. "I'm just thinking about that day, man.
Her in the dress. All these niggas watching me try not to cry." "Cry,
then." He stared. "I'm bringing sunglasses," I added. "For both of
us." Tank laughed and leaned his shoulder against mine.

54
00:20:18,510 --> 00:20:40,252
Across the room, Rosa saw us standing together and sang the next line
straight at him. My brother smiled like the whole war could wait until
she finished. Vesper returned from the terrace carrying a folded note.
She laid it beside my plate. "From Roland." I opened it. TOMORROW. THE
EMPEROR'S TABLE. COME WITHOUT YOUR ARMY.

55
00:20:40,502 --> 00:21:02,898
Underneath, in a different hand, someone had added: I HAVE NEVER SEEN
ANYONE DRIVE LIKE THAT. I passed it to Shawna. She read both lines and
shook her head. "Your enemies keep writing you fan mail." "Niggas
recognize a man doing his job." She kissed me in front of the whole
room. Outside, the Last City lit up another thousand windows.

56
00:21:03,148 --> 00:21:18,984
Somewhere behind them, Zero was planning my death. Inside our
ballroom, my friends began arguing about which car should carry Tank
and Rosa away from their wedding. I listened to them like a man
listening to his future arrive early.
